_np = [['Set a written par level for every item, for example 20 units of a fast seller and 4 of a slow one, so anyone on shift can see at a glance what counts as low.', 'Review the five costliest items weekly and the rest monthly, because a few lines usually hold most of the money tied up in stock.'], ['Give every item one unique code and one description, such as BOLT-M8-50 for a box of fifty, so the same product is never counted under two names.', 'Record each shelf, bin and bay with a short label like A-02-3 and mark it physically, so a new team member can find any item in under a minute.'], ['Run a cycle count on one aisle each day instead of a full yearly shutdown, comparing the shelf to the record and noting every difference above two units.', 'Investigate each difference by checking the last three receipts and issues before changing the number, and write the cause beside the correction.'], ['Use the last twelve months of sales by month, then adjust for known events such as a seasonal peak, to estimate next month rather than copying last month alone.', 'Compare forecast with actual each month and calculate the gap as a percentage; if you miss by more than 20 percent twice, change the method.'], ['Calculate reorder point as daily usage times supplier lead time plus a safety margin; for example 6 a day times 5 days plus 12 spare gives a trigger of 42.', "Write the rule on the shelf label or in the sheet, with the order quantity and the supplier, so the reorder does not depend on one person's memory."], ['Store heavy and fast-moving goods between knee and shoulder height, and keep a clear aisle of at least one metre so picking is quick and safe.', 'Use first in, first out by placing newer deliveries behind older ones and marking dates on cartons, so older stock leaves before it expires or fades.'], ['Flag any item with no movement in 90 days and list it with its cost; decide to discount, bundle, return or write it off within a set month.', 'Track shrink as a percentage of stock value each month, and compare breakage, miscount and theft causes so you fix the largest cause first.'], ['Build a one-page monthly report with stock value, turnover, stock-outs and shrink, and keep the same layout each month so trends are easy to see.', 'Choose one improvement per month, such as fixing the top five stock-outs, state a target number and check the result at the next review.']]
C['mods'] = [(m[0], m[1], list(m[2])+_np[i], m[3], m[4], m[5]) for i, m in enumerate(C['mods'])]
C['intro_add'] = ['You will set par levels and review the costliest items first, seeing stock as tied-up cash that deserves regular attention.', 'You will give each item one code and each shelf one label, so every product has a single name and a findable place.', 'You will run daily cycle counts of one aisle and trace the cause of each difference, keeping records trustworthy without a shutdown.', 'You will forecast next month from twelve months of sales, adjust for known events, and measure the miss so the method improves.', 'You will calculate a reorder point from usage, lead time and safety stock, and write the rule where staff can follow it.', 'You will arrange shelves by weight and speed and apply first in, first out, cutting damage, expiry loss and picking time.', 'You will flag items with no movement in ninety days and measure shrink by cause, deciding what to discount, return or write off.', 'You will build a one-page monthly stock report and pick one improvement with a target, so owners see the picture and trends.']
